1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,996
Here's our reference motor again: twelve
slots, eight magnet poles.

2
00:00:05,036 --> 00:00:10,084
Between the magnets and the stator teeth
is an air gap just half a millimetre wide.

3
00:00:10,124 --> 00:00:14,574
The magnets' flux crosses it twice, and
runs through centimetres of steel.

4
00:00:14,614 --> 00:00:18,021
Yet, after the magnet itself, it's that
thin layer of air,

5
00:00:18,061 --> 00:00:20,501
not the steel, that sets the field.

6
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:22,793
Today we'll see why, and predict that
field

7
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:25,465
from a magnet datasheet and two lengths.

8
00:00:26,765 --> 00:00:28,913
First, last lesson's answers.

9
00:00:28,953 --> 00:00:33,769
One: a coil of N turns carrying I makes an
MMF step of N·I,

10
00:00:33,809 --> 00:00:37,132
a square wave from −N·I/2 to +N·I/2.

11
00:00:37,172 --> 00:00:39,724
Two: balanced three-phase currents give a

12
00:00:39,764 --> 00:00:42,969
rotating fundamental 3/2 of one phase's
peak.

13
00:00:43,009 --> 00:00:48,916
Three: with ideal iron, B in the gap is
μ₀F/g, so doubling the gap halves it.

14
00:00:48,956 --> 00:00:51,849
Today we'll see what 'ideal iron' really
means.

15
00:00:53,149 --> 00:00:57,283
Take a steel ring with a coil of N turns,
and cut a narrow gap in it.

16
00:00:57,323 --> 00:01:02,391
Around the centre line of the core,
Ampère's law says the line integral

17
00:01:02,431 --> 00:01:07,899
of H equals the enclosed current, N·I: the
magnetomotive force, or MMF.

18
00:01:07,939 --> 00:01:11,459
Field lines never end, so if no flux
escapes, the flux,

19
00:01:11,499 --> 00:01:15,290
B times the area, is the same in every
cross-section.

20
00:01:15,330 --> 00:01:19,171
In each part, H is B over μ. So N·I equals
the flux,

21
00:01:19,211 --> 00:01:22,979
times a sum of terms: length over μ times
area.

22
00:01:23,019 --> 00:01:24,976
Each term is a reluctance.

23
00:01:26,276 --> 00:01:29,867
That's Ohm's law in magnetic form: MMF
plays voltage,

24
00:01:29,907 --> 00:01:33,829
flux plays current, and reluctance plays
resistance.

25
00:01:33,869 --> 00:01:38,592
Reluctances in series add, and high
permeability means low reluctance.

26
00:01:38,632 --> 00:01:43,212
In 1886, John and Edward Hopkinson used
magnetic-circuit equations

27
00:01:43,252 --> 00:01:47,201
to predict a dynamo's characteristic from
its dimensions.

28
00:01:47,241 --> 00:01:49,911
Designers still start this way.

29
00:01:51,211 --> 00:01:56,100
Let's put in numbers: 200 turns at 2 amps,
400 ampere-turns.

30
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:59,263
A steel path about 320 millimetres long,

31
00:01:59,303 --> 00:02:03,695
relative permeability 4000, and a
1-millimetre gap.

32
00:02:03,735 --> 00:02:10,477
B in the gap is 0.47 tesla, and the gap
alone takes 93 percent of the MMF.

33
00:02:10,517 --> 00:02:13,871
Shrink the gap and B rises roughly as one
over g.

34
00:02:13,911 --> 00:02:17,690
But at about a quarter of a millimetre it
passes 1.5 tesla,

35
00:02:17,730 --> 00:02:21,984
the steel saturates, and this linear model
stops applying.

36
00:02:23,284 --> 00:02:27,199
Here's why. Divide each length of steel by
its relative permeability:

37
00:02:27,239 --> 00:02:30,465
that's the length of air with the same
reluctance.

38
00:02:30,505 --> 00:02:34,731
Unroll the core: 319 millimetres of steel
shrink to

39
00:02:34,771 --> 00:02:39,581
0.08 millimetres of air, next to a
1-millimetre gap.

40
00:02:39,621 --> 00:02:44,073
With good steel, the gap sets the field.
That's what 'ideal iron' meant.

41
00:02:44,113 --> 00:02:48,382
But μr isn't constant. Near saturation it
can fall to a few hundred,

42
00:02:48,422 --> 00:02:50,696
and the steel's share grows.

43
00:02:51,996 --> 00:02:56,296
The analogy has a weak spot: there's no
practical insulator for flux.

44
00:02:56,336 --> 00:03:00,911
Air carries flux only a few thousand times
less readily than steel.

45
00:03:00,951 --> 00:03:04,851
Here's a two-dimensional finite-difference
solution of the same core,

46
00:03:04,891 --> 00:03:08,164
with a 6-millimetre gap to make the effect
visible.

47
00:03:08,204 --> 00:03:10,223
Some flux leaks across the window,

48
00:03:10,263 --> 00:03:14,621
and around the gap the field bulges
outward: leakage and fringing.

49
00:03:14,661 --> 00:03:18,742
B at the centre of the gap still matches
the circuit within 1 percent,

50
00:03:18,782 --> 00:03:23,970
because Ampère's law along the centre line
doesn't care where the other flux goes.

51
00:03:24,010 --> 00:03:27,360
But the coil drives more flux than the
circuit predicts:

52
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:31,593
a quarter more with a 1-millimetre gap,
twice as much with 6.

53
00:03:32,893 --> 00:03:35,718
Now replace the coil with a permanent
magnet.

54
00:03:35,758 --> 00:03:39,218
Driving flux through a gap, it works in
the second

55
00:03:39,258 --> 00:03:43,118
quadrant of its B–H curve: B positive, H
negative.

56
00:03:43,158 --> 00:03:48,633
For sintered neodymium, that's nearly a
straight line,

57
00:03:48,673 --> 00:03:52,607
the recoil line: B = Br + μ₀μrec H.

58
00:03:52,647 --> 00:03:58,304
We'll use Br = 1.2 T and μrec = 1.05,
barely more than air.

59
00:03:58,344 --> 00:04:02,374
The line crosses B = 0 near −910 kA/m.

60
00:04:02,414 --> 00:04:05,606
It only holds above a knee. Push the
magnet past it,

61
00:04:05,646 --> 00:04:10,310
more easily when it's hot, and some
magnetisation is lost for good.

62
00:04:11,610 --> 00:04:15,407
Put a magnet in series with a gap, and
take the steel as ideal.

63
00:04:15,447 --> 00:04:19,646
With no current, Ampère's law gives zero:

64
00:04:19,686 --> 00:04:25,318
Hm·hm + Hg·g = 0. So the magnet runs at
negative H.

65
00:04:25,358 --> 00:04:27,646
With equal areas, B is the same in both,

66
00:04:27,686 --> 00:04:31,555
which gives a straight line through the
origin: the load line.

67
00:04:31,595 --> 00:04:35,111
Its slope is set by the permeance
coefficient:

68
00:04:35,151 --> 00:04:37,985
hm over g, times the area ratio.

69
00:04:38,025 --> 00:04:40,660
The magnet works where the two lines
cross.

70
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:44,543
Close the gap, and the operating point
climbs toward Br.

71
00:04:44,583 --> 00:04:48,478
A 5-millimetre magnet and a 2-millimetre
gap:

72
00:04:48,518 --> 00:04:52,691
permeance coefficient 2.5, and 0.85 tesla.

73
00:04:53,991 --> 00:04:57,554
Now the reference motor. Follow one loop
of magnet flux:

74
00:04:57,594 --> 00:05:00,547
out of a north magnet, across the gap,
down a tooth,

75
00:05:00,587 --> 00:05:03,827
along the stator yoke, up the next tooth,
across the gap,

76
00:05:03,867 --> 00:05:07,507
through a south magnet, and home through
the rotor steel.

77
00:05:07,547 --> 00:05:13,164
Each magnet is an MMF source, Hc·hm, with
an internal reluctance.

78
00:05:13,204 --> 00:05:17,032
Gaps and steel are reluctances in series.

79
00:05:17,072 --> 00:05:21,373
By symmetry, each pole sees one magnet,
one gap, and half the steel.

80
00:05:22,673 --> 00:05:25,635
Magnets 3 millimetres thick, a half-
millimetre gap,

81
00:05:25,675 --> 00:05:28,172
and two refinements to the flat model.

82
00:05:28,212 --> 00:05:30,006
The flux spreads out with radius,

83
00:05:30,046 --> 00:05:33,862
so the gap's area is almost 10 percent
larger than the magnet's.

84
00:05:33,902 --> 00:05:38,782
And the steel adds only 0.015 millimetres
of equivalent air.

85
00:05:38,822 --> 00:05:42,147
The result: about 0.94 tesla in the gap.

86
00:05:42,187 --> 00:05:46,837
An exact radial solution agrees within 0.2
percent.

87
00:05:46,877 --> 00:05:49,740
Teeth and yoke then carry about 1.5 tesla,

88
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:53,595
near the knee, so linear steel is
optimistic here.

89
00:05:53,635 --> 00:05:58,492
Leakage between magnets and the slot
openings lower the gap field further.

90
00:05:58,532 --> 00:06:02,208
Lesson 5 computes them with a 2D field
solution.

91
00:06:03,508 --> 00:06:08,008
Check it on the bench. Put an N38 block
magnet, 5 millimetres thick,

92
00:06:08,048 --> 00:06:13,215
on one jaw of a steel C-yoke; steel plates
on the other jaw set the gap.

93
00:06:13,255 --> 00:06:17,519
Slide a transverse Hall probe, flat, into
the middle of the gap.

94
00:06:17,559 --> 00:06:22,761
Probes are 1 to 1.5 millimetres thick, so
start with a 2-millimetre gap.

95
00:06:22,801 --> 00:06:28,938
The circuit predicts 0.85 tesla at 2
millimetres, and 0.49 at 7.

96
00:06:28,978 --> 00:06:33,847
A 2D solution with mild steel and fringing
expects 3 to 7 percent less,

97
00:06:33,887 --> 00:06:37,704
and your magnet's own remanence scales
everything.

98
00:06:37,744 --> 00:06:43,047
Zero the meter away from magnets, and keep
fingers clear: the plates snap on hard.

99
00:06:44,369 --> 00:06:46,649
Three predictions before next time.

100
00:06:46,689 --> 00:06:52,879
One: flat geometry, ideal iron. A
3-millimetre magnet, Br = 1.2 T,

101
00:06:52,919 --> 00:07:00,782
μrec = 1.05, drives a half-millimetre gap.
What's the flux density in the gap?

102
00:07:02,082 --> 00:07:05,642
Two: double the magnet's thickness. What
does B become?

103
00:07:06,942 --> 00:07:10,898
Three: a 40-millimetre steel path with μr
= 4000

104
00:07:10,938 --> 00:07:15,101
is worth how much air gap? And with μr =
40?

105
00:07:16,401 --> 00:07:22,013
Next time: inside the steel itself, with
saturation, hysteresis and eddy currents.
